  "dom_id": "step-q139553",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14094829/assets/f9b7a55ee1b8d3327ae9da740feaafde459daaee4e42a27cf26fd0ac8e63f20b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,\u2016x\u2016=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of ${A}^{T}A?$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,\u2026,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge \u2026\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $\u22ef$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=6={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={6}^{2}=36,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=2={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={2}^{2}=4.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=36$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$",
